{"qid":"Q1069690","names":["Chen"],"title":"Chen","endonym":null,"capsule":"Chen was a Zhou-period Chinese state occupying the central plain of present-day Henan province, with its capital at Wanqiu (near modern Huaiyang). Founded as a fief granted to Gui Man, a descendant of the legendary Emperor Shun, at the start of the Western Zhou, it survived as one of the smaller Spring and Autumn period polities through diplomatic maneuvering between the dominant powers of Chu and Jin. Repeatedly invaded and briefly annexed by Chu, Chen was finally extinguished by Chu in 479 BCE. Its ruling house gave its clan name to the Chinese surname Chen (陳).","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The state is attested in the Zuo Zhuan and Shiji with reasonable chronological precision, but territorial boundaries are reconstructed from later scholarship and remain approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Chen (state)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chen_(state)"},{"label":"Britannica: Spring and Autumn period","url":"https://www.britannica.com/event/Spring-and-Autumn-period"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Chen (state)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Duke Wen of Chen","nativeName":"陳文公","roman":"Chen Wen Gong","start":-754,"end":-745,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Duke Huan of Chen","nativeName":"陳桓公","roman":"Chen Huan Gong","start":-744,"end":-707,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Tuo of Chen (Duke Li)","nativeName":"陳厲公佗","roman":"Chen Li Gong Tuo","start":-706,"end":-700,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense"